WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.126
The core idea is to represent the classification
problem as a conflict hypergraph.

00:00:05.206 --> 00:00:06.904
Each vertex is a data point,

00:00:06.984 --> 00:00:12.300
and a set of points forms a hyperedge when they
belong to different classes yet share

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.713
overlapping adversarial neighborhoods,

00:00:14.793 --> 00:00:19.220
meaning an attacker could push any of them to the
same confusing input.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:25.188
The optimal 0-1 loss then becomes a linear program:
maximize the probability mass of correctly

00:00:25.268 --> 00:00:30.648
classified points subject to constraints encoded by
the hypergraph's incidence matrix.

00:00:30.728 --> 00:00:34.965
The dual program is a fractional covering of
vertices by hyperedges,

00:00:35.045 --> 00:00:38.521
and it directly yields the optimal adversarial strategy.

00:00:38.601 --> 00:00:42.584
Because computing all hyperedges up to degree ten
is infeasible,

00:00:42.664 --> 00:00:47.663
the authors truncate the hypergraph to lower
degrees for tractable lower bounds,

00:00:47.743 --> 00:00:51.790
aggregate binary one-versus-one losses for an even
cheaper bound,

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:55.980
and apply a generalized Caro-Wei bound for a
matching upper bound.
